1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:00,762
Welcome.

2
00:00:00,812 --> 00:00:06,945
This is the product recap of the Beijing security conference held on the third and fourth of November 2026.

3
00:00:06,995 --> 00:00:15,556
It is built for the Chief Product Officer and the leadership team, and it covers every product ecosystem we discovered, who uses it, who pays for it,

4
00:00:15,606 --> 00:00:19,081
how the value chain works, and what it means for our roadmap.

5
00:00:19,131 --> 00:00:25,668
The material behind this video is nine documents: six AI-generated transcripts of booth and meeting conversations,

6
00:00:25,718 --> 00:00:30,985
two strategy infographics prepared after day one, and the NUCTECH millimeter-wave datasheet.

7
00:00:31,035 --> 00:00:37,572
One warning before we begin: the transcripts are noisy and were never checked against audio, so vendor identities,

8
00:00:37,622 --> 00:00:42,137
prices and performance claims are treated as signals to validate, not as facts.

9
00:00:42,187 --> 00:00:50,690
The thesis that runs through the whole recap is simple: our opportunity is to move from selling security devices to delivering connected operations,

10
00:00:50,740 --> 00:00:56,295
where every alert becomes actionable, assigned and auditable on the ISMP V11 core we already own.

11
00:00:57,595 --> 00:00:59,585
Here is how the video is organised.

12
00:00:59,635 --> 00:01:03,257
Part one draws the ecosystem map and the operating value chain.

13
00:01:03,307 --> 00:01:10,602
Part two goes deep on the four products our leadership flagged as top priority: smart glasses, millimeter-wave body screening,

14
00:01:10,652 --> 00:01:13,808
facial multi-parameter AI, and fence intrusion sensing.

15
00:01:13,858 --> 00:01:18,938
Part three covers the connected-operations signals from the security and compute booths.

16
00:01:18,988 --> 00:01:21,736
Part four covers identity and controlled assets.

17
00:01:21,786 --> 00:01:28,090
Part five distils the intelligence-layer know-how from the vertical large-model session at Peking University.

18
00:01:28,140 --> 00:01:31,237
Part six parks the adjacent categories on a watchlist.

19
00:01:31,287 --> 00:01:39,223
And part seven is the synthesis for the Chief Product Officer: stakeholders, prioritisation, product model, roadmap, the ninety-day plan,

20
00:01:39,273 --> 00:01:42,371
the decisions each executive must take, and the risks.

21
00:01:43,671 --> 00:01:45,137
Let us start with the map.

22
00:01:45,187 --> 00:01:48,109
Everything we met in Beijing fits into five layers.

23
00:01:48,159 --> 00:01:56,503
At the bottom is frontline sensing and wearables: smart patrol glasses with and without cameras, a patrol watch, millimeter-wave body screening,

24
00:01:56,553 --> 00:02:03,965
fence force and vibration sensing, biometric capture devices, passport readers, and smart gun cabinets with ultra-wideband tags.

25
00:02:04,015 --> 00:02:12,067
Above it is edge AI and compute: edge nodes that pull video from third-party recorders, cameras with built-in compute trained per industry,

26
00:02:12,117 --> 00:02:14,923
heterogeneous compute servers, and managed racks.

27
00:02:14,973 --> 00:02:19,936
The middle layer is platform and orchestration, which is where our own ISMP V11 lives,

28
00:02:19,986 --> 00:02:26,698
alongside response plans that drive drones and unmanned vehicles, digital twins, and integrated building management.

29
00:02:26,748 --> 00:02:33,227
The intelligence layer holds vertical large models, MCP agents, facial attribute AI, and text search over video.

30
00:02:33,277 --> 00:02:37,249
And at the top are adjacent categories we should watch but not pilot.

31
00:02:37,299 --> 00:02:45,409
Keep this map in mind: our strategic position is the platform layer, and every lower-layer product is a candidate sensor or actuator for it.

32
00:02:46,709 --> 00:02:49,944
The second idea to hold onto is the operating value chain.

33
00:02:49,994 --> 00:02:52,946
Value is not created when a sensor detects something.

34
00:02:52,996 --> 00:03:02,179
It is created when the detection is verified with evidence, assigned to an accountable owner, resolved with a confirmed outcome, and audited in a traceable record.

35
00:03:02,229 --> 00:03:07,503
Almost every booth we visited sells the detect step: a camera, an algorithm, a scanner, a box.

36
00:03:07,553 --> 00:03:10,108
Very few connect it to assignment and closure.

37
00:03:10,158 --> 00:03:17,585
In fact, vendors themselves told us that the Chinese market is component-driven, highly competitive, and weak on system integration.

38
00:03:17,635 --> 00:03:20,020
That is exactly the gap our platform fills.

39
00:03:20,070 --> 00:03:27,893
Throughout this video, when we assess a product, the question is always: where does it plug into detect, verify, assign, resolve and audit,

40
00:03:27,943 --> 00:03:30,499
and what does it add to the complete workflow?

41
00:03:31,799 --> 00:03:33,716
Product one: smart patrol glasses.

42
00:03:33,766 --> 00:03:34,873
We saw two flavours.

43
00:03:34,923 --> 00:03:39,908
Camera glasses stream a first-person view to a command centre and run face recognition.

44
00:03:39,958 --> 00:03:45,984
Display-only glasses, with no camera, show prompts, watch-lists, a teleprompter or live data on the lens,

45
00:03:46,034 --> 00:03:50,092
and are controlled by a single tap to confirm and a double tap to exit.

46
00:03:50,142 --> 00:03:56,110
The why is hands-free capture and prompting for people who walk: patrol officers, guards and inspectors.

47
00:03:56,160 --> 00:04:03,806
The who includes public-security and traffic police who need long shifts, private security companies, inspectors, and remote experts.

48
00:04:03,856 --> 00:04:09,362
The where is patrol routes, checkpoints and campuses, with the feed landing in a command centre.

49
00:04:09,412 --> 00:04:15,148
Civilian versions are already on Taobao for vloggers, which tells you the hardware is commoditising.

50
00:04:15,198 --> 00:04:20,067
The when is now: demo units exist and the vendor suggested we buy one or two to test.

51
00:04:20,117 --> 00:04:28,689
The how matters most for us: Bluetooth pairing to a phone or standalone Android with mobile device management, prescription lenses fitted per wearer,

52
00:04:28,739 --> 00:04:29,615
and, critically,

53
00:04:29,665 --> 00:04:37,600
the vendor can hand us the original image so that we do feature extraction and matching in our own backend and only the result flows back.

54
00:04:38,900 --> 00:04:39,841
What is our role?

55
00:04:39,891 --> 00:04:41,474
We should not build glasses.

56
00:04:41,524 --> 00:04:50,684
We partner for the terminal and the base SDK, and we own the task definition, the SOP, the evidence chain and the audit, reusing the platform we already have.

57
00:04:50,734 --> 00:04:59,370
The product flow is: capture on site, run one targeted detection matched to one job, confirm with a human on the lens, and create a task in ISMP V11.

58
00:04:59,420 --> 00:05:08,115
The commercial hypothesis is terminal plus industry workflow licence plus training and support, always benchmarked against a phone doing the same job.

59
00:05:08,165 --> 00:05:16,160
Concerns raised on the floor were cyber-security of pushing video to a backend, sovereignty of face features, and real-shift battery life.

60
00:05:16,210 --> 00:05:23,738
Before any pilot, the CTO needs a sample unit to answer: which model and camera, which streaming interfaces, where the model runs,

61
00:05:23,788 --> 00:05:27,061
what the offline behaviour is, and who controls the data.

62
00:05:27,111 --> 00:05:35,106
The COO should accept only on task results: completion time, first-time completion rate, usable-image rate, and actual frontline adoption.

63
00:05:35,156 --> 00:05:39,420
Only if a repeatable operational gain over the phone appears do we expand.

64
00:05:40,720 --> 00:05:43,182
Product two: millimeter-wave body screening.

65
00:05:43,232 --> 00:05:48,778
The what is non-contact detection of items hidden in or under clothing, metallic and non-metallic,

66
00:05:48,828 --> 00:05:53,003
with automatic threat recognition and a privacy-preserving cartoon figure.

67
00:05:53,053 --> 00:05:56,943
The why is replacing pat-downs and the stop-and-raise-your-arms pose.

68
00:05:56,993 --> 00:06:04,137
The booth pitch was: bind the person's face, walk through, the result appears, and the officer gives pass or no-pass feedback.

69
00:06:04,187 --> 00:06:04,651
Who pays?

70
00:06:04,701 --> 00:06:08,819
The airport's equipment department, not the airlines and not immigration.

71
00:06:08,869 --> 00:06:13,444
Other buyers are customs, border, rail transit, prisons and government buildings.

72
00:06:13,494 --> 00:06:14,643
Where is it deployed?

73
00:06:14,693 --> 00:06:23,208
Beijing, Shanghai Pudong, Xinjiang, Tianjin and Wuhan in China, three units in Nigeria, and none yet in Southeast Asia, which is an open field for us.

74
00:06:23,258 --> 00:06:28,347
The when is now, and the vendor is actively going to Dubai and Amsterdam to find partners.

75
00:06:28,397 --> 00:06:30,517
The how is active holographic imaging.

76
00:06:30,567 --> 00:06:38,682
The MW1000AA datasheet lists a two-second scan and a four-second inspection with a brief stand, while the booth unit claims continuous walking.

77
00:06:38,732 --> 00:06:41,765
That gap is the single most important thing to verify.

78
00:06:41,815 --> 00:06:51,072
Field pain points we heard: metal plates in shoes still trigger alarms, European height requirements, and queues when screening is pushed to the building entrance.

79
00:06:52,372 --> 00:06:57,018
The buying decision for millimeter wave hinges on certification, not image quality.

80
00:06:57,068 --> 00:07:03,638
The vendor holds only the Chinese civil-aviation approval at the highest national level, with no TSA or ECAC listing.

81
00:07:03,688 --> 00:07:09,975
They said Singapore sits in the middle: it wants the price but also the certification, and that is the headache.

82
00:07:10,025 --> 00:07:17,103
The vendor itself is a radar company with three lines: body screening, tank-level radar for petrochemicals, and traffic radar,

83
00:07:17,153 --> 00:07:21,573
built on its own sixty-five nanometre chips, which are expensive at low volume.

84
00:07:21,623 --> 00:07:25,024
They offer remote diagnostics and a self-check at every boot.

85
00:07:25,074 --> 00:07:26,835
For us there are four judgments.

86
00:07:26,885 --> 00:07:31,022
Detection boundary, tested separately for metallic and non-metallic items.

87
00:07:31,072 --> 00:07:36,906
Lane efficiency, measured as real peak throughput, nuisance alarms and re-check time for the whole lane.

88
00:07:36,956 --> 00:07:42,620
Users and deployment, including passenger guidance and an explainable alarm location for the officer.

89
00:07:42,670 --> 00:07:51,785
And the ST product and revenue model, linking the scanner to identity, gates and ISMP V11 and testing a device plus lane integration plus lifecycle service offer.

90
00:07:51,835 --> 00:07:58,801
The gate before any commercial introduction is a site acceptance with customer-approved target items and the operating flow.

91
00:08:00,101 --> 00:08:02,430
Product three: facial multi-parameter AI.

92
00:08:02,480 --> 00:08:07,650
Leadership saw a demo that reads identity, age, expression and blood pressure from a face.

93
00:08:07,700 --> 00:08:12,987
The product discipline here is to split it into four capabilities and judge each separately.

94
00:08:13,037 --> 00:08:21,515
Identity verification is mature: liveness detection is now standard on every Chinese face camera, and face plus QR is the most popular combination.

95
00:08:21,565 --> 00:08:27,374
We align it with reader and gate flows and accept on false-accept, false-reject and live performance.

96
00:08:27,424 --> 00:08:33,813
Age estimation is an apparent range with error, fine for validated analytics but never for a boundary decision.

97
00:08:33,863 --> 00:08:41,470
Expression analysis classifies visible expression; the day-two demo tagged emotion, gender, mask, glasses, hand in pocket and smile.

98
00:08:41,520 --> 00:08:45,531
The output is not inner emotion, and we must not infer danger from it.

99
00:08:45,581 --> 00:08:52,318
Blood-pressure estimation is probably video-based photoplethysmography, but the vendor did not confirm the mechanism.

100
00:08:52,368 --> 00:08:56,495
It needs an independent reference-device study and a clear intended use.

101
00:08:56,545 --> 00:08:57,771
It is not a diagnosis.

102
00:08:57,821 --> 00:09:07,401
We also learned that iris cameras cost around three thousand RMB and sell poorly, while palm-vein cameras are cheaper but niche, used only for classified-unit access.

103
00:09:08,701 --> 00:09:10,573
That gives us two validation paths.

104
00:09:10,623 --> 00:09:14,089
Path A takes identity straight into our existing access control.

105
00:09:14,139 --> 00:09:20,846
We reuse our identity management and gate workflows, take the original image from the vendor and do the matching ourselves,

106
00:09:20,896 --> 00:09:25,735
and accept on false-accept and false-reject rates under real lighting, masks and glasses.

107
00:09:25,785 --> 00:09:28,482
Path B treats health estimation as evidence-first.

108
00:09:28,532 --> 00:09:35,348
We need validity against a reference device with independent subjects, robustness across lighting, skin tone, age and motion,

109
00:09:35,398 --> 00:09:41,061
and a clear statement of intended use before we look at target-market regulation, consent and liability.

110
00:09:41,111 --> 00:09:49,521
The hard rule for the whole company: until that evidence exists, health estimates never enter a safety release decision or an automated disposition chain.

111
00:09:49,571 --> 00:09:58,585
For the Chief Marketing Officer, this means packaging identity, entrance and perimeter value separately, and describing health features strictly within the evidence.

112
00:09:59,885 --> 00:10:02,603
Product four: fence force and vibration sensing.

113
00:10:02,653 --> 00:10:11,253
The product essence is detecting an intrusion attempt through the force, deformation or vibration a fence experiences, then turning that into a zoned,

114
00:10:11,303 --> 00:10:13,041
located and dispatchable event.

115
00:10:13,091 --> 00:10:19,153
The sensing method, whether tension, sensing cable or fibre, still has to be confirmed with the exhibitor.

116
00:10:19,203 --> 00:10:27,111
The value chain is disturbance, sensing and processing with on-site calibration, zone alarm with location, video verification in ISMP V11,

117
00:10:27,161 --> 00:10:32,128
and dispatch with closure and audit, potentially with the patrol glasses feed attached.

118
00:10:32,178 --> 00:10:40,201
Acceptance must cover three cases: real intrusions, measured on detection, location error and latency; non-intrusion disturbances like wind,

119
00:10:40,251 --> 00:10:48,332
rain and animals, measured as nuisance alarms per kilometre per day; and equipment and link faults, measured on visibility and recovery time.

120
00:10:48,382 --> 00:10:54,099
Our role is to partner for the sensor and own zoning, video linkage, event flow, dispatch and audit,

121
00:10:54,149 --> 00:10:58,193
after checking the overlap with the perimeter products we already sell.

122
00:10:58,243 --> 00:11:06,093
The commercial test is sensor plus installation and calibration plus platform linkage plus maintenance, proven on one real fence segment.

123
00:11:07,393 --> 00:11:11,526
Now the connected-operations signals from the security and compute booths.

124
00:11:11,576 --> 00:11:13,956
Signal one is NVR-connected edge analytics.

125
00:11:14,006 --> 00:11:21,135
A vendor showed an edge node that connects to existing third-party network video recorders, Dahua was named, pulls the streams,

126
00:11:21,185 --> 00:11:28,483
and lets the operator search video with text for a person or a trajectory, running at around sixteen times speed on a small model.

127
00:11:28,533 --> 00:11:34,079
The why is powerful: it adds intelligence to the installed camera estate without replacing cameras.

128
00:11:34,129 --> 00:11:37,018
The who is control-room operators and investigators.

129
00:11:37,068 --> 00:11:39,788
The when is demo-ready but not fully productised.

130
00:11:39,838 --> 00:11:44,366
For us, this is the prove-first pilot of the whole strategy: video plus incident.

131
00:11:44,416 --> 00:11:47,362
The edge node becomes the sensor for the verify step.

132
00:11:47,412 --> 00:11:54,201
The proof we need is which recorders and APIs are supported, the retrieval quality, the latency, and the cost per stream.

133
00:11:55,501 --> 00:11:57,525
Signal two is response orchestration.

134
00:11:57,575 --> 00:12:06,044
One vendor described a control layer that can command any embodied terminal: cameras, robots, unmanned vehicles and drones, with pre-set response plans.

135
00:12:06,094 --> 00:12:12,042
Their example: a camera detects a fight, a drone flies to the zone, captures faces, and performs dispersal.

136
00:12:12,092 --> 00:12:20,057
Another booth showed a patrol watch that receives alerts and short videos, with a voice command on the watch triggering an action on the phone.

137
00:12:20,107 --> 00:12:25,606
The buyer motivation was stated clearly: management and public security care about the closed loop,

138
00:12:25,656 --> 00:12:29,978
and leaders want the first eighty percent of routine work removed from people.

139
00:12:30,028 --> 00:12:38,273
The product implication for us is to make incident ownership, dispatch and closure the reusable workflow, with terminals interchangeable underneath.

140
00:12:38,323 --> 00:12:46,848
The proof we need is live versus staged dispatch, acknowledgement and retries, proof of closure, and clear rules of engagement for any autonomous action.

141
00:12:48,148 --> 00:12:51,590
Signal three is digital twin and integrated building management.

142
00:12:51,640 --> 00:12:57,810
A vendor showed a 3D-model platform used to place devices before a building is built and to operate it afterwards,

143
00:12:57,860 --> 00:13:03,539
with an operations centre showing surrounding construction, schedules and all connected building systems.

144
00:13:03,589 --> 00:13:09,105
Their IBMS controls ventilation, drainage, chillers and lighting through DDC controllers and gateways.

145
00:13:09,155 --> 00:13:16,143
They serve factories, construction sites and hospitals across government and industry, and the group already has an overseas arm.

146
00:13:16,193 --> 00:13:24,218
For us the implication is to connect incidents to assets and locations, and to partner for the specialist building functions rather than build them.

147
00:13:24,268 --> 00:13:33,493
The proof needed is a live rather than recorded demo, the asset identifiers and data model, read and write access to building points, and the integration effort per site.

148
00:13:34,793 --> 00:13:37,022
Signal four is managed compute and racks.

149
00:13:37,072 --> 00:13:45,360
We saw smart power racks that report temperature, humidity, power draw and compute status to a central platform, with cabinet doors that open by face,

150
00:13:45,410 --> 00:13:46,472
card or fingerprint.

151
00:13:46,522 --> 00:13:55,032
They are air-cooled, sealed, with cold and hot aisles, and the largest deployments are airports and an aviation command centre, in the thousands of units.

152
00:13:55,082 --> 00:14:02,147
Hikvision has an AI box small enough to sit inside a drone dock, which tells you edge compute is spreading to very small scenes.

153
00:14:02,197 --> 00:14:10,151
The product implication is that infrastructure belongs in our deployment and service economics, sized to a measured workload rather than a demo.

154
00:14:10,201 --> 00:14:18,989
For the Chief Financial Officer: hardware, cooling, power and support are a recurring line in every connected-operations deal, and the rack decides the margin.

155
00:14:20,289 --> 00:14:25,544
Some compute economics were quoted on the floor, and every figure is a quote to verify.

156
00:14:25,594 --> 00:14:30,118
A Huawei eight-card AI server was priced around seven hundred thousand RMB.

157
00:14:30,168 --> 00:14:36,704
Domestic chips were claimed to reach seventy to eighty percent of an NVIDIA 3090 or 4090 after optimisation.

158
00:14:36,754 --> 00:14:40,058
Two 5090 cards were around fifteen and a half thousand.

159
00:14:40,108 --> 00:14:45,851
A twenty-six-billion-parameter model was said to cost twenty to thirty thousand dollars to run.

160
00:14:45,901 --> 00:14:52,559
Vertical-model vendors run private GPUs because customers refuse cloud, and our customers behave the same way.

161
00:14:52,609 --> 00:14:58,230
The token-based AI apps we saw, virtual try-on and digital humans, live or die by token cost.

162
00:14:58,280 --> 00:15:06,706
The rule for our product decisions: benchmark the real workload across edge and on-prem options on quality, latency, uptime and total cost.

163
00:15:06,756 --> 00:15:16,768
And keep the roles clear: AI assists with evidence and drafts, accountable operators control dispatch, access and closure, and economics are measured, never assumed.

164
00:15:18,068 --> 00:15:21,943
The edge-AI vision vendors we met organise their algorithms by industry.

165
00:15:21,993 --> 00:15:24,396
Public security wants face and licence plate.

166
00:15:24,446 --> 00:15:27,722
Safety production wants helmet, vest and behaviour detection.

167
00:15:27,772 --> 00:15:31,320
Construction wants twenty-four-hour supervision of compliant work.

168
00:15:31,370 --> 00:15:39,824
Cameras ship with front-end compute, custom models are trained from collected images and pushed to the device, and a backend platform aggregates everything.

169
00:15:39,874 --> 00:15:43,913
The money is moving: public-security budgets have been tight for two years,

170
00:15:43,963 --> 00:15:51,164
so vendors are chasing central state-owned enterprises such as the power grids, and safety-production regulation driven by accidents.

171
00:15:51,214 --> 00:15:53,617
For us, this split mirrors our buyers abroad.

172
00:15:53,667 --> 00:15:57,160
Industry model packs are a partner input, not something to build.

173
00:15:57,210 --> 00:16:03,212
And demand follows regulation and incidents, so the roadmap should align to compliance triggers in each market.

174
00:16:03,262 --> 00:16:11,280
One more observation: domestic security construction in China has peaked, and government projects are hardware-heavy with little money for software,

175
00:16:11,330 --> 00:16:13,897
which is why these vendors are looking overseas.

176
00:16:15,197 --> 00:16:17,619
Part four: identity and controlled assets.

177
00:16:17,669 --> 00:16:26,035
On biometric capture we saw dual-finger, single-finger and rolled fingerprint capture, four-finger slap for visas and airports, and palm print,

178
00:16:26,085 --> 00:16:31,038
with references in prisons in Turkey and a Brazilian state driving-licence programme.

179
00:16:31,088 --> 00:16:34,098
For us these are components to source, not to build.

180
00:16:34,148 --> 00:16:44,103
Passport readers offer OCR of the data page and chip decoding in about three seconds, with no data retention by default because the law requires it, and multilingual OCR.

181
00:16:44,153 --> 00:16:46,340
Face is the default modality in China.

182
00:16:46,390 --> 00:16:50,519
Face plus QR is the common pairing, and liveness is standard and cheap.

183
00:16:50,569 --> 00:16:54,050
Iris cameras cost around three thousand RMB and sell poorly.

184
00:16:54,100 --> 00:16:58,111
Palm vein is cheaper but niche, used only for classified-unit access.

185
00:16:58,161 --> 00:17:02,760
Our identity line should therefore treat face, QR and card as tiered assurance,

186
00:17:02,810 --> 00:17:11,177
source fingerprint and palm capture for border and licensing programmes, and keep iris and palm vein as options for the highest-security rooms.

187
00:17:12,477 --> 00:17:16,164
Gun and ammunition management is a regulated asset-control product.

188
00:17:16,214 --> 00:17:22,858
Smart cabinets have per-weapon locks, cabinet types per weapon class, a master controller that decides which lock opens,

189
00:17:22,908 --> 00:17:26,038
and face authentication before an officer draws a weapon.

190
00:17:26,088 --> 00:17:34,239
A national standard governs anywhere guns are stored, permits are verified, and the data is reported to national level, not just to the department.

191
00:17:34,289 --> 00:17:39,761
Users are police stations down to the smallest county town, and army and armed-police sentry posts.

192
00:17:39,811 --> 00:17:48,185
The off-post alarm is clever and cheap: an ultra-wideband tag inside the weapon and a base unit at the post, detecting leaving rather than positioning,

193
00:17:48,235 --> 00:17:50,695
with a range of roughly three hundred metres.

194
00:17:50,745 --> 00:17:57,780
For us this is the same product pattern as the key, cabinet and rack management we already run for military and critical sites,

195
00:17:57,830 --> 00:18:00,514
and a repeatable compliance-driven buying motion.

196
00:18:00,564 --> 00:18:08,491
Validation means confirming the standards in each target market, integration to our identity and audit, and real duty-cycle tests for the tags.

197
00:18:09,791 --> 00:18:11,616
Part five: the intelligence layer.

198
00:18:11,666 --> 00:18:19,008
On the afternoon of day two we met a Peking University research institute that deploys vertical large models in medicine and industry.

199
00:18:19,058 --> 00:18:20,828
Their paradigm is worth adopting.

200
00:18:20,878 --> 00:18:24,800
Start from the customer's scenario and knowledge base, not from a model.

201
00:18:24,850 --> 00:18:30,481
Build an expert-confirmed evaluation set first, which they called the biggest human value contribution.

202
00:18:30,531 --> 00:18:35,556
Measure, then diagnose where points are lost: retrieval, summarisation or complex reasoning.

203
00:18:35,606 --> 00:18:39,472
Govern the data, cleaning noise and conflicts with graphs and ontology.

204
00:18:39,522 --> 00:18:44,988
Then keep runtime errors, fix without touching the model where possible, and fine-tune periodically.

205
00:18:45,038 --> 00:18:52,434
They were clear that fine-tuning is not the default answer to a five percent error, and that open-source retrieval alone is not enough:

206
00:18:52,484 --> 00:18:54,420
the system around it is the product.

207
00:18:54,470 --> 00:18:59,440
They also argued that medicine and industry differ far less than in the digitalisation era,

208
00:18:59,490 --> 00:19:06,721
and that predictive maintenance is already at eighty-five to ninety percent, so the value is an LLM interface for frontline workers.

209
00:19:06,771 --> 00:19:09,699
Their speed is striking: systems in one to two months.

210
00:19:09,749 --> 00:19:17,091
And in private deployment they use Qwen, DeepSeek, Mistral for Europe and Zhipu for state enterprises, because customers refuse cloud.

211
00:19:18,391 --> 00:19:20,471
We asked the lab a direct question:

212
00:19:20,521 --> 00:19:28,079
we want to expose our access-control functions as MCP services so robots and agents can open and close doors by conversation,

213
00:19:28,129 --> 00:19:30,453
knowing this is critical and dangerous.

214
00:19:30,503 --> 00:19:37,453
Their guidance: command-type operations belong on APIs via MCP, and search should be reserved for complex analysis.

215
00:19:37,503 --> 00:19:46,218
Single tool calls are solved; the hard problem is tool selection when many capabilities combine, and search accuracy is bounded by MCP accuracy.

216
00:19:46,268 --> 00:19:54,070
Most importantly, they recognised that our access rules, who can enter, when, which room, contractors, weekends, are an ontology,

217
00:19:54,120 --> 00:19:59,305
and that outside vendors cannot replicate it because they do not understand our rules.

218
00:19:59,355 --> 00:20:08,009
That is why ST is hard to copy: eleven generations over thirty years, un-clonable cards and secured readers, critical-infrastructure customers,

219
00:20:08,059 --> 00:20:10,809
and the cyber-security discipline others skip.

220
00:20:10,859 --> 00:20:17,261
The product principle: agents request, the ontology and policy engine decide, and every action is audited.

221
00:20:18,561 --> 00:20:21,319
The dialogue also produced strategic insights.

222
00:20:21,369 --> 00:20:30,047
First, generic platforms are the last era: ontology or model-building platforms sold to every industry are a dead end except for frontier labs;

223
00:20:30,097 --> 00:20:32,610
build vertical and own your own knowledge.

224
00:20:32,660 --> 00:20:35,052
Second, integration must become product.

225
00:20:35,102 --> 00:20:38,043
System integration has no profit in the long run,

226
00:20:38,093 --> 00:20:45,428
so thirty years of integration knowledge must be crystallised into ontology and product so each integration gets cheaper.

227
00:20:45,478 --> 00:20:54,949
Third, small teams and fast loops: ten to twenty people, proposals in a day, proofs of concept in one to two months, and avoid slow state-enterprise cycles.

228
00:20:54,999 --> 00:21:00,809
Fourth, talent and co-operation: large-model PhDs cost around three million RMB a year in China,

229
00:21:00,859 --> 00:21:06,912
so package non-AI-heavy development to capable Chinese teams and do AI-deep work with an expert lab,

230
00:21:06,962 --> 00:21:10,086
starting with one themed workshop for our engineers.

231
00:21:10,136 --> 00:21:20,035
Fifth, geography: use Chinese brains for overseas terminals; Singapore is the gateway to ASEAN, and our critical-infrastructure markets do not compete with theirs.

232
00:21:20,085 --> 00:21:26,627
Sixth, everything is private and on-prem, and cyber-security discipline is both a cost and a differentiator.

233
00:21:27,927 --> 00:21:31,398
Part six: adjacent categories we should watch but not pilot.

234
00:21:31,448 --> 00:21:38,030
A companion robot for the elderly with a camera, emergency calling and a psychology layer on top of a base model,

235
00:21:38,080 --> 00:21:44,017
priced around one hundred thousand RMB at the show; its buyer is families and eldercare, not security.

236
00:21:44,067 --> 00:21:52,527
AI virtual try-on that swaps clothing, including uniforms by rank, in thirty seconds, sold to clothing stores and live commerce on a token model.

237
00:21:52,577 --> 00:21:59,746
Digital-human guides, demonstrated as a Su Dongpo persona, aimed at museums and reception, moving to on-prem token servers.

238
00:21:59,796 --> 00:22:07,259
And humanoid or embodied robots, where even vendors admitted that most robots still lack the brain and the brain is still human.

239
00:22:07,309 --> 00:22:14,947
The classification rule is simple: an adjacent product earns a pilot only when it adds a measurable step to detect, verify, assign,

240
00:22:14,997 --> 00:22:17,823
resolve and audit for a paying security operator.

241
00:22:19,123 --> 00:22:20,595
Part seven: the synthesis.

242
00:22:20,645 --> 00:22:22,176
First, the stakeholder map.

243
00:22:22,226 --> 00:22:28,498
For each product, the user, the buyer and the influencer are different people, and our position differs too.

244
00:22:28,548 --> 00:22:38,275
Glasses are used by officers and guards, bought by police forces and security firms, gated by IT security and HR, and we partner for the terminal and own the workflow.

245
00:22:38,325 --> 00:22:46,705
Millimeter wave is used by screening officers, bought by airport equipment departments, customs, rail and prisons, gated by aviation regulators,

246
00:22:46,755 --> 00:22:50,452
and we integrate device, lane and ISMP with a lifecycle service.

247
00:22:50,502 --> 00:22:58,473
Facial AI is bought by access-control owners for identity, while the health features have no defined buyer until the intended use is set.

248
00:22:58,523 --> 00:23:06,376
Fence sensing is bought by perimeter owners such as utilities, ports, camps and airports, and we own zoning, video, dispatch and audit.

249
00:23:06,426 --> 00:23:10,767
Video plus incident is our prove-first pilot with existing recorder owners.

250
00:23:10,817 --> 00:23:15,450
Orchestration is bought by security operations leaders who want the closed loop.

251
00:23:15,500 --> 00:23:18,963
Digital twin and racks share buyers with the analytics deal.

252
00:23:19,013 --> 00:23:23,822
Biometric capture and passport readers are bought by border and licensing agencies.

253
00:23:23,872 --> 00:23:29,325
And gun cabinets extend the key and cabinet management we already sell to police and military.

254
00:23:30,625 --> 00:23:36,600
Second, prioritisation, plotted as strategic value against the evidence the floor actually proved.

255
00:23:36,650 --> 00:23:45,084
Top right, high value and high evidence, is where we prove first and then scale: video plus incident on ISMP V11 using NVR edge analytics,

256
00:23:45,134 --> 00:23:50,125
response orchestration from assignment to audit, fence intrusion as a zoned event,

257
00:23:50,175 --> 00:23:54,675
and the vertical-model paradigm of evaluation sets and private deployment.

258
00:23:54,725 --> 00:24:01,253
Top left, high value but low evidence, is where we validate fast: smart glasses on one job against a phone,

259
00:24:01,303 --> 00:24:08,200
millimeter wave on walk-through and certification, MCP agents for access control, and facial identity into gates.

260
00:24:08,250 --> 00:24:17,422
Bottom right, lower value but mature, is where we partner or source: digital twin and IBMS, racks and compute, biometric capture and passport readers,

261
00:24:17,472 --> 00:24:20,742
and gun cabinets as an extension of our existing line.

262
00:24:20,792 --> 00:24:27,812
Bottom left is the watchlist: facial blood pressure and expression, companion robots, humanoids and virtual try-on.

263
00:24:29,112 --> 00:24:30,600
Third, the product model.

264
00:24:30,650 --> 00:24:38,907
We own the platform core: one incident record and one operating model, with the event schema, permissions, incident state, audit trail,

265
00:24:38,957 --> 00:24:46,353
the identity and access ontology, and versioned integration contracts, reusing ISMP V11 and building only validated gaps.

266
00:24:46,403 --> 00:24:55,275
We configure the solution layer: SOPs, severity rules, escalation paths, dashboards, asset context and the industry model packs a customer needs.

267
00:24:55,325 --> 00:25:03,459
And we adapt to the customer environment through six adapter families: video and analytics, access and building systems, field tools,

268
00:25:03,509 --> 00:25:06,782
entrance screening, perimeter, and specialist systems.

269
00:25:06,832 --> 00:25:09,182
Devices and algorithms are replaceable.

270
00:25:09,232 --> 00:25:15,704
The event interface, the disposition responsibility, the evidence and the service experience stay with ST.

271
00:25:15,754 --> 00:25:23,826
Product acceptance always includes auditability, failure recovery, offline behaviour, customer data rules and a named support owner.

272
00:25:25,126 --> 00:25:26,274
Fourth, the roadmap.

273
00:25:26,324 --> 00:25:33,761
Now, in the first three months, we validate: pilot video plus incident on one existing campus using the customer's recorders;

274
00:25:33,811 --> 00:25:37,355
test a glasses sample on one job with a phone control group;

275
00:25:37,405 --> 00:25:45,979
run the millimeter-wave walk and stop demo with target items and lane throughput while mapping the certification path; survey one fence segment;

276
00:25:46,029 --> 00:25:53,586
test facial identity at one gate while keeping health features evidence-only; and run an MCP access-control spike with the lab,

277
00:25:53,636 --> 00:25:55,083
starting with a workshop.

278
00:25:55,133 --> 00:26:04,068
Next, from three to twelve months, we extend: connected patrol across phone, watch and glasses; fence zone events wired to video presets and dispatch;

279
00:26:04,118 --> 00:26:11,794
a millimeter-wave lane at one checkpoint if certification is credible; compute sized from measured workload; partner model packs;

280
00:26:11,844 --> 00:26:14,849
and our own evaluation set for operator assistance.

281
00:26:14,899 --> 00:26:21,916
Later, in twelve to twenty-four months, we partner and platform: incidents linked to digital twin and building assets,

282
00:26:21,966 --> 00:26:29,582
agent-ready access control under a policy engine, an entrance product family combining identity, screening and passport capture,

283
00:26:29,632 --> 00:26:36,710
controlled-item cabinets, and regional expansion into Southeast Asian airports and Middle East critical infrastructure.

284
00:26:38,010 --> 00:26:40,217
Fifth, the ninety-day validation plan.

285
00:26:40,267 --> 00:26:42,771
Days one to thirty: demand and feasibility.

286
00:26:42,821 --> 00:26:50,017
Interview three buyers or operators, choose one workflow and baseline it, and confirm vendor SDK access and written scope.

287
00:26:50,067 --> 00:26:52,511
The gate is an agreed pilot specification.

288
00:26:52,561 --> 00:26:55,124
Days thirty-one to sixty: pilot and measure.

289
00:26:55,174 --> 00:27:01,658
Connect one site in assisted mode and measure the complete task, including exceptions and frontline usability.

290
00:27:01,708 --> 00:27:03,855
The gate is comparable pilot results.

291
00:27:03,905 --> 00:27:06,231
Days sixty-one to ninety: business case.

292
00:27:06,281 --> 00:27:11,458
Compare outcomes, total cost and repeat-deployment effort, and define the service model.

293
00:27:11,508 --> 00:27:13,477
The gate is scale, revise or stop.

294
00:27:13,527 --> 00:27:21,792
In parallel we bring back four evidence cards: glasses versus phone on one task, millimeter-wave walk and stop results with lane throughput,

295
00:27:21,842 --> 00:27:28,800
split facial specifications with a blood-pressure reference study, and a fence survey with intrusion and weather logs.

296
00:27:28,850 --> 00:27:38,541
The packaging to test is a platform and workflow licence plus integration plus annual support, and for devices, device plus lane integration plus lifecycle service.

297
00:27:39,841 --> 00:27:41,032
Sixth, the decisions.

298
00:27:41,082 --> 00:27:48,778
The CEO confirms one use case, the sponsor and gated funding, and brings back a named pilot customer with agreed decision criteria.

299
00:27:48,828 --> 00:27:58,356
The CTO owns the architecture spike and technical vendor validation, including the MCP security model, and brings back integration proof and a workload benchmark.

300
00:27:58,406 --> 00:28:04,563
The CMO owns discovery, positioning and packaging, selling outcomes supported by evidence, with entrance,

301
00:28:04,613 --> 00:28:09,293
patrol and perimeter packaged separately and health claims kept within evidence.

302
00:28:09,343 --> 00:28:16,979
The COO owns pilot operations and service readiness: SOPs, response accountability, nuisance-alarm handling and support ownership.

303
00:28:17,029 --> 00:28:24,547
The CFO sets the investment cap and the economics gate, covering hardware, compute, licensing, support and token versus on-prem,

304
00:28:24,597 --> 00:28:29,809
and brings back total cost per site and the margin model for device plus service bundles.

305
00:28:29,859 --> 00:28:37,318
The Chief Commercial Officer owns partner and channel strategy: SDK and API terms with the glasses, scanner and sensor vendors,

306
00:28:37,368 --> 00:28:42,225
the certification roadmap with the scanner vendor, co-operation terms with the lab,

307
00:28:42,275 --> 00:28:47,487
and regional entry into Southeast Asian airports and Middle East critical infrastructure.

308
00:28:47,537 --> 00:28:52,040
Product Management coordinates the pilot specification and the decision pack.

309
00:28:52,090 --> 00:28:59,075
The decision proposed is to sponsor a scoped validation and return with measured operator value and a go or no-go case.

310
00:29:00,375 --> 00:29:02,474
Seventh, success criteria and risks.

311
00:29:02,524 --> 00:29:08,624
Value is measured in minutes per incident, acknowledgement and closure time, and avoidable repeat work.

312
00:29:08,674 --> 00:29:13,101
Quality is false and missed alerts, task completion and frontline adoption.

313
00:29:13,151 --> 00:29:19,668
Economics is integration days, total cost of hardware, compute, licensing and support, and willingness to pay.

314
00:29:19,718 --> 00:29:25,339
We scale only with measured operator benefit, viable economics and a repeatable delivery model.

315
00:29:25,389 --> 00:29:30,175
The risks: the evidence itself is noisy; the scanner lacks Western certification;

316
00:29:30,225 --> 00:29:35,727
video and face data leaving devices raise sovereignty and cyber questions; health estimation,

317
00:29:35,777 --> 00:29:42,772
expression inference and autonomous drone actions carry regulatory exposure; compute and token costs can erase margin;

318
00:29:42,822 --> 00:29:50,353
the scanner's chip supply is low-volume; and the organisational shift from an integration mindset to a product mindset is real.

319
00:29:50,403 --> 00:29:55,368
Everything here is a proposal to validate, not a demonstrated end-to-end capability.

320
00:29:56,668 --> 00:29:58,626
To close, the one-page takeaway.

321
00:29:58,676 --> 00:30:01,890
Make every alert actionable, assigned and auditable.

322
00:30:01,940 --> 00:30:04,651
Prove video plus incident on ISMP V11 first.

323
00:30:04,701 --> 00:30:10,237
Validate glasses, millimeter wave, facial identity and fence sensing with evidence cards.

324
00:30:10,287 --> 00:30:13,061
Partner for digital twins, racks and sensors.

325
00:30:13,111 --> 00:30:16,638
Adopt the evaluation-set discipline for every AI feature.

326
00:30:16,688 --> 00:30:20,906
And keep agents behind the policy engine, with every action audited.

327
00:30:20,956 --> 00:30:22,726
That is the recap of Beijing.

328
00:30:22,776 --> 00:30:32,202
Use it to align the CTO, CMO, COO, CFO and CCO on one scoped validation, and return in ninety days with measured operator value and a go or no-go case.

329
00:30:32,252 --> 00:30:32,830
Thank you.
